WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.940
So, how did you become the Voice of the Tigers?

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.160
Were you the first man to be . . . ?

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.860
No. The first, I think, to be called the Voice of the Tigers.

4
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:20.520
But there had been two or three announcers of LSU football games prior to me.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:29.100
When I came here, the job was open and so I auditioned for the job and won the audition.

6
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.620
So beginning in 1946, I broadcast the LSU football games.

7
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:41.600
First games in Tiger Stadium and in that run, at that time, I did the games for three consecutive years.

8
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:51.900
Then in 1961, I started doing the LSU games again and did them until the middle eighties, without interruption.

9
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.720
Okay.

10
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.300
Is that when you became to be called the Voice of the Tigers?

11
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.520
Yeah.

12
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:09.000
In that period, beginning in ’61 or along in there, Walter Hill, who was my color announcer and commercial announcer,

13
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:18.380
and also is still a very good friend and a talented man, one day just referred to me as “Voice of the Tigers.”

14
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.860
And I guess it stuck.

15
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.940
We never did make too much of that.

16
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.620
He always introduced me as the Voice of the Tigers.

17
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:35.740
And of course, as I say, the designation stuck and then was used all the way through.

18
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:41.640
As a matter of fact, even though I don’t do the games any longer, they still use it for the guy who does the games now.
